ments  with us in this study find almost 2  500 new mutations that have a growth  effect  upon peripheral toxicity  we can select the most prominent hits  which were enriched and thus showed the  highest fall changes between control and  peripheral treatment  based on this data we can now select our  most promising strain  revalidate it and use this as the  starting strain for next round of  diversity generation  for example by combining the winners or  simply do a second round of genome-wide  agnostic mutations  so we can perform combinatorial  optimization  this way we can quickly jump the fitness  ladder and dramatically increase  tolerance towards toxic compounds  or increase the titer of desired product  if that's what you want to optimize  it's of course also possible to do both  and we've actually done so  in a project where we optimized lysine  production  and looked for increased tolerance  against thialysin which is a toxic  biometabolite  in the lysine amino acid biosynthesis  pathway  so today i showed you data where we  designed and generated more than 25 000  precise and trackable edits  across seven genome scale libraries in a  really easy and intuitive way  we performed growth selection of these  libraries in 11 industrially relevant  conditions  we characterized more than 800 000 data  points  for quality and broad response patterns  we performed genotype phenotype maps  of response to inhibitory compounds  fertile and nanoli  and this study we confirmed existing  literature as well as discovered  hundreds of novel targets  for strain improvement  i want to now show you how easy it is to  create high throughput massively  parallel genome engineering libraries  with the onyx platform in your own lab  inscriptors onix platform supports all  areas of the design  generate test and learn cycle our online  software  called inscriptor designer will design  guide rna and donor dna pairs and assign  a unique barcode  you simply inform the software what  edits you want and where you want them  we've built an intuitive graphical user  interface  and if you are more bioinformatic savvy  you can also upload a csv  fireball order edit you want inscriptive  designer will provide a color code for  each design  based on more than 1 000 different  parameters  in order to guide you in relation to how  good each particular design  is you can then select the designs you  want and test  and we will manufacture the custom  reagents containing your specific guide  rna  donor dna and barcode in complete kits  to  drive genome engineering you will then  receive  the custom reagents and the  microfluidics consumables  which fit into the onyx benchtop  instrument that you have in your lab  the footprint of the instrument is very  small the instrument will take care of  all wetland parts of the editing  experiment  the on instrument editing takes 2-3 days  and the result is a heterogeneous cell  population  of minimum 1 times 10 to the 7th cell  we've developed ngs essays for both  immediate qc of the editing experiment  and for downstream analysis of the  population after phenotyping  once phenotyped it's possible to track  back any barcode sequencing to the  genome level  and upload this data into our online  encrypted resolver software  which will help you analyze experimental  results and thus guide you for the next  round of the gtl  cycle  i want to pause here and just explain to  you how inscriptor only calls  edits if they are complete and intended  you see here on the left panel an  example of what we mean by complete and  intended  in this experiment we wanted to change  the codon for tyrosine tac  to threonine act so even though we still  encode a treonum with aca  it's complete but it's not intended and  therefore not included in the metric  you can also see that we create  auxiliary edits in the pam and spacer  regions  to prevent recutting of the mat 7  endonuclease  here you see on the circus plot it's  just an example of one single guard rna  targeting chromosome 1 of a yeast genome  and with the height of the bar  indicating the editing rate and thus the  fraction of cells in the population  with a complete and intended edit on  that specific position  when comparing different metrics with  literature it's important to think about  what is meant by that metric  typically mechanisms of both  non-homologous and joining and homology  directed repair  are repo are reported as successful  editing  but when you move from single edits to  mass parallel libraries of guide rnas  we need to be able to exactly trust that  the edit we designed is also what we see  in the final population  therefore we only count edits repaired  by homology directed repair  at this specified genome position so  each guide will dependent on precision  and edit type behave a little  differently so an inscriptor reported  edit efficiency is the number of cells  in the cell population  which has a complete and intended edit  whereas the design is observed is  showing the number of designs we find in  the population  i'll just give you an example of what  these metrics mean  if you have fifty percent of yourself in  the library with complete unintended  edit  and the 99 design represented in the  library  of ten thousand designs we can per one  million output cells  expect to find five hundred thousand  cells in the population with a complete  and intended edit  of those 500 000 cells we will find  9900 editing designs at least  once  so here you see the specs of the onyx  platform the technology has been  developed for e coli in yeast  it's optimized for inscriptive standard  strains and we can also use custom  strains on the instrument  we perform one edit per cell and those  can be  insertions deletions and substitutions  and a combination  combination of those in the libraries  each library can generate from 100  to 10 000 edits for e coli and up to six  thousand edits for yeast  so if you want more edits as we did in  the data i just showed you  it's possible to pool cells from  different libraries and phenotype  everything together  the added efficiency is thirty but  varies from 15 to  75 depending on the specific library  produced  dna based editing libraries and other  reagent manufacturing  is three to four weeks and the on  instrument time is only two to three  days  so i just i want to end here by thanking  all my brilliant colleagues in the  applications development and product  development teams  who designed the experiment did the lab  work and  all the data analysis  and i just want to thank you for  attention and i'll be happy to take  any questions  thank you dr clinton for your  informative presentation  we will now start the live q a portion  of our webinar  now to our audience if you have any  questions you would like to ask  please do so now just click on the ask a  question box  located on the far left of your screen  and we will answer as many of your  questions as we have time for  okay let's get started we have lots of  questions already coming in  dr clinton let's start with this first  one when tracking  genome edits using the plasmid barcode  how confident are you that the edit has  been made  so thanks uh christy um that's a great  question  um we are quite certain that the edits  are made but of course you cannot be 100  sure uh because uh as i said in the  presentation that  the barcode stays on the plasmid we  don't want the barcodes on  genome so we recommend to actually  reconfirm  that you have that genomic edit  especially if you have some interesting  strains that you  that you are looking for and you can do  that with whole genome sequencing you  can also do amplicon sequencing of that  particular  region if it's just a few edits but you  can actually we also developed a  pooled whole genome sequencing essay for  um  to actually do the whole pool  so it makes it really easy to see the  genomic edits  um yeah  very good thank you our next question  would it be possible to explain in a bit  more detail  how your editing protocol allow for the  generation of both a  high variety of edits and across the  genome  yeah so this is a basically very kind of  this  question is very kind of core uh  of the technology because that's  actually what onyx is about  it's to have that ability to do both and  the and that's why we've developed this  holistic  way of like end-to-end solution because  you need  very good guide rna design that's very  important to  to really get that and you need the  homology arm you need that to happen  very efficiently but you also need to  make sure that you're not uh  you know um introducing something that  you don't want  so we worked a long time actually to  develop this technology  so that's why we have the instrument  that is running in the lab and we have  the microfluidics components to make  sure that we do  that we are able to design these  thousands of edits in one library  and that that we can do both the  substitutions and the  insertions and the deletions and you can  basically  you can basically mix that up into every  library  and because this is working in single  cells that's the reason why  you can actually do this  thank you dr clinton our next question  are cells alive post-experiment  can you isolate them again for  large-scale fermentation  and how is the dna extracted for library  prep  for illumina seek yeah so  yes they are alive uh because we are  working with living cells and that's  what  this is all about uh and yes you can  also isolate them  afterwards so you can grow them up as  clonal isolates  and yes to actually be able to  to get that specific clone and grow that  up  for any like other experiments for  example if you want to do  uh like the next round of diversity  generation you can use that uh  strain and um it's also possible to  like if you want to do con if you want  to confirm your edits  you can actually also prepare like  create a small li